            <ArticleTitle>Multi-disciplinary research can be more than parallel-disciplinary research: the case of GPs' use of magnetic resonance imaging.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>301-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We aimed to show how in multi-disciplinary research data collected to meet the needs of one discipline can provide information of value to another.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Using the critical incident technique, 25 GPs were interviewed about recent scans requested for patients with knee and lumbar spine complaints. Transcripts of the interviews were scrutinized from both a medical and an economic perspective.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Five key economic issues where further research is needed were identified.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The total value of the information provided by multi-disciplinary research may exceed the sum of the information collected to meet the requirements of the individual disciplines.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
